October 27 2026
Finished the schematic for the 7.4V to 5V buck regulator and 5V to 3.3V LDO.
Added a UVLO to prevent battery degredation and oscillation. Von was set as 6.85V and Voff was set as 6.10V.
Added test points near the input voltage to measure how much was coming from the battery 
  and on the other half to measure if the buck regulator was outputting 5V.
Thinking about how the battery is going to connect to the board. It can either be soldered or solder the JST-SYP male 
  pin to the board. For now I think soldering the JST-SYP male pin will be easier since it allows me to detach the battery, but it might
  be a design decision for me to decide later after I have an idea of the physical design of the drone.
At the end of the day I added the decoupling capacitors to the VDD pins on the STM32, with a low pass filter and decoupling capacitor for
  VDDA. However, I still don't really understand what decoupling capacitors and low pass filters work. Why they're required, when we know
  they should be used and which low pass filters fit each occasion. I hope to mostly study the theory behind those sometime later.

October 28 2026
For the most part worked on the schematic for STM32 and learned a bit about low pass filters, decoupling capacitors and when they're needed.
  Decided that instead of having an LC filter for my low pass filter from 3.3V to VDDA I added a ferrite bead. Additionally, I added a 
  decoupling capacitor for my VBAT which I didn't realize I had to. 
Adding decoupling capacitors for both VCAP1 and VCAP2, finished the schematic for the BOOT0 pin. 
I also finished the schematic for the HSE crystal oscillator. I wan't to study these a bit more in the future since I feel I don't have that
  good of an idea of how they work, but currently I view it as sort of a time keeper for the MCU.
In terms of theory I learned why a low pass filter is needed between the buck regulator, LDO and MCU. Based on my understanding it's that the 
  buck regulator has a ton of switching, causing spikes in frequency. And the PSRR on the LDO struggles with high frequencies. I still don't
  have a solid idea of how all that works, so I want to study it in the future with how all the components work.
